\paragraph{Revised measurement protocol.}\label{sec:revised-protocol}
The Llama-3.2-1B rows of Table~\ref{tab:master-wikitext} were re-measured under a stricter protocol that removes four confounds we found in the earlier campaign. First, thermal state: every cell starts genuinely cold (CPU and DDR below 37\,\textdegree C, shell below 34\,\textdegree C), which takes about 26 minutes of idle in a 24\,\textdegree C room once accumulated heat has soaked into the chassis. A warm start silently activates the watchdog and costs about 20\% of decode throughput. Second, power: charging stays disabled for the whole run so the state of charge is constant, and energy integrates total system power (USB rail plus battery discharge) split at the prefill/decode boundary. Integrating the USB rail alone mis-attributes the battery's share. Third, cache accounting: the retained-KV footprint is measured by \texttt{llama\_state\_seq\_get\_size}, which serializes live cells only. Position-range counters over-state it. Fourth, baseline fidelity: every baseline runs its own paper's configuration with none of $\mu$KV's mechanisms. SnapKV uses the per-head FasterDecoding defaults (window 64, avgpool-5, top-$(K{-}64)$ from the prefix, no sinks); Ada-KV uses the Ada-SnapKV defaults (window 32, maxpool-7, $\alpha{=}0.2$ safeguard, no sinks); H2O keeps its 50/50 heavy-hitter and recent split from accumulated attention with no sinks; StreamingLLM keeps its own 4 attention sinks plus recent window; TOVA scores from the last query, in both the per-layer variant and the strict canonical form. The AdaKV row predates this audit (it ran with a 16-query window and 4 protected sinks) and is being re-measured under the exact Ada-SnapKV configuration. The watchdog runs on $\mu$KV only and stayed dormant from cold. The vendor thermal engine's first skin clamp (1632 to 1498\,MHz near 41\,\textdegree C) engaged identically in every cell, so the comparison is unaffected; the deeper mitigations never engaged. $\mu$KV-mass-full combines FA-on prefill (in-graph scoring on the last chunk) with a 290\,ms post-eviction defrag, so it gets vanilla-class prefill and state-swap-class decode at once.

Two caveats. PPL here is teacher-forced on a disjoint WikiText continuation. It rewards retained context and is inflated by memorization for large caches (vanilla's 1.06 reflects recall of memorized text), so it over-states $\mu$KV's continuation cost; self-generation PPL is about 1.1 for every policy. The pass scores the reference one token at a time and each policy runs its own eviction schedule during scoring: one-shot policies keep their frozen post-prefill selection, while TOVA and H2O evict per step as their papers specify, so the protocol is uniform and each algorithm runs as designed. The second caveat is that $\mu$KV's decode-time eviction (2.5K evictions over the 4K decode) bounds the cache near 2K cells, above the nominal $K{=}1024$, while every baseline grows without bound. We report the measured mean rather than the nominal budget.

\paragraph{Reading the figures.} Figure~\ref{fig:cache-trajectory} shows the mechanism: every policy builds the full prompt cache during prefill; at the end of prefill $\mu$KV compacts to 721 cells and its decode-time eviction keeps the cache bounded near 2K, while SnapKV can only drop to 5.9K cells and vanilla grows without bound. Figure~\ref{fig:main-results} shows what the bounded cache buys: 3.6$\times$ vanilla throughput at 52\% less energy with the smallest retained cache and the coolest peaks. Figure~\ref{fig:thermal-trace} shows the thermal consequence: $\mu$KV finishes in 10 minutes while vanilla runs 21 and climbs toward the throttle line; the shell stays at the watchdog threshold only briefly, so the watchdog stays dormant, and with the 1632\,MHz cap the kernel throttle condition is never reached in these cells (uncapped runs do reach it, which is where the watchdog thresholds come from). The clock trace has two separate behaviors. The variation below the ceiling is the stock DVFS governor tracking load on a memory-bound workload. The ceiling itself drops once: the vendor thermal engine clamps the big cores from 1632 to 1498\,MHz when the shell reaches about 41\,\textdegree C, in every cell alike, writing the frequency limit directly so the cpufreq cooling state stays zero. This clamp onset independently reproduces the throttle thresholds we measured on uncapped runs. $\mu$KV finishes about four minutes after the clamp engages; vanilla runs fourteen of its twenty-two minutes under it. Vanilla's gradual clock rise late in the run is also the governor: its cache grows without bound, each token does more work, and utilization rises. The clock is smoothed over 30 samples.

