\paragraph{Measurement notes for Table~\ref{tab:phone-niah-prelim}.} The prompt embeds the needle at depths $d\in\{0,17,33,50,67,83,100\}\%$ of context; a hit means the 64-token generation contains the needle. Wall is the median end-to-end time per cell and is prefill-dominated on this short-decode workload, so higher decode throughput does not lower wall here; the 2K-decode workload of Table~\ref{tab:master} flips that ordering. ActKV is the end-of-prefill attended set. Temperatures are median peaks across depths because means skew on single-cell outliers (one H2O cell triggered a BCL event with a 93\,\textdegree C prime-core spike). One Gemma-2B 8K $\mu$KV-mass cell logged a 10.5\,Wh USB-rail reading at normal wall and throughput, a clear sensor glitch, and is excluded from that energy mean.

\paragraph{Why $\mu$KV-mass is the recommended default over $\mu$KV-count.} The two variants differ only in how the dispatcher computes $\alpha_a$: count-$\alpha$ uses the binary argmax-position metric (Sec.~\ref{sec:design} the dispatcher v1), mass-$\alpha$ uses the column-mean attention partition (the dispatcher v2). On every response-generation workload measured (chat, QA, NIAH), the count-$\alpha$ saturates at 0.95 across all prompts --- it is functionally static. The mass-$\alpha$ varies smoothly per prompt: 0.62--0.78 for short chat, 0.78--0.92 for medium QA, 0.81--0.91 for long retrieval. Operationally, mass-$\alpha$ produces 40\,\% smaller active KV (250 vs 416 at 4K NIAH on Phi-3) at the same NIAH hit rate (7/7 vs 7/7), 4\,\% higher decode throughput (5.7 vs 5.5), and 2--3\,\textdegree C lower DDR temperature. The compute cost is identical (the same column-mean reduction anchor selection already requires for anchor ranking). We therefore deploy mass-$\alpha$ as the production default and retain count-$\alpha$ only as an ablation in Sec.~\ref{sec:eval-tdak}.

\subsection{Cache size is not a temperature actuator}
\label{sec:eval-tdak}

We also tested coupling the cache budget itself to DDR temperature (a four-tier ladder over $K\in\{256,512,768,1024\}$, symmetric and ratcheted variants, under cool and co-loaded regimes). The mechanism works, but the result is negative and we do not ship it: cache size controls throughput, wall time, and energy, not peak temperature. Halving $K$ under co-load left peak DDR unchanged because the co-load dominates the thermal budget, and on a cool phone a smaller cache runs hotter at peak because the saved bandwidth converts to speed and power. Frequency remains the only lever that caps heat, which is why the shipped design pairs eviction (efficiency) with the clock watchdog (temperature) and nothing else.

\subsection{Watchdog: gradual clock reduction before the kernel throttle}
\label{sec:eval-thermal}

The watchdog is a userspace daemon that polls skin, battery, DDR, and CPU sensors at 2\,Hz, maps each to a normalized threat against thresholds calibrated from measured pre-throttle events, and reduces the big-core frequency cap one step at a time (1632, 1497, 1382, 1267\,MHz) as the maximum threat rises, with hysteresis on the way back down. It never raises the clock and runs only under $\mu$KV. From a cold start it stays dormant (Table~\ref{tab:master-wikitext}); its purpose is to replace the kernel's reactive cliff (a single drop to 883\,MHz) with a small preventive reduction. Its thresholds come from real throttle events we measured on uncapped runs. In the capped cold-start campaign the platform's own first mitigation tier did engage in every cell, a skin-triggered clamp from 1632 to 1498\,MHz at about 41\,\textdegree C; the deeper tiers (the cpufreq cooling device and the 883\,MHz cliff) never did.


\subsection{Cross-model replication of the throughput-energy Pareto}
\label{sec:eval-cross}

The headline Phi-3 result replicates on Llama-3.2-1B and gemma-2-2b-it (Table~\ref{tab:master}, bottom 6 rows). On Llama-1B at 16K decode, $\mu$KV reaches \good{16.29\,tps} (vs vanilla 4.87, AdaKV 5.90) at \good{446\,mWh} system energy (vs vanilla 634, AdaKV 653). On gemma-2-2b, \good{7.17\,tps} (vs vanilla 3.35, AdaKV 2.69) at \good{847\,mWh} (vs 1322, 1367). The pattern is consistent: $\mu$KV is the fastest and the lowest-energy policy on every model, holds DDR 8--10\,\textdegree C below the cliff vanilla and AdaKV approach, and uses the least RSS. PPL on Llama-1B and Gemma-2B shows the count-$\alpha$ saturation effect (Sec.~\ref{sec:design}, the dispatcher v1) over-allocating anchors for smaller models; the mass-$\alpha$ dispatcher rebalances toward recent on these regimes, closing the quality gap in follow-on sweeps.

\subsection{Limitations and scope}
\label{sec:limitations}

\emph{Device.} All measurements on a single OnePlus 15 (Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5). Watchdog thresholds are device-specific; the calibration protocol (10 measured pre-throttle events on the vanilla baseline) generalizes but the specific tier values do not.
\emph{Energy.} System energy via USB rail $V\cdot I$ integration with charging disabled throughout each cell. This is total-device energy (CPU, DDR, modem, screen-off); we cannot isolate DDR-specific energy without a die-attached power meter.


\paragraph{Why CPU-only is the right comparison (not a concession to the GPU).} A natural objection is that our numbers ignore the mobile GPU/NPU. They do not: they reflect what \emph{any} attention-driven evictor can use. Every score-reading policy in Table~\ref{tab:master} (H2O, TOVA, AdaKV, $\mu$KV) needs post-softmax attention, which fused GPU/NPU kernels never materialize --- so the entire eviction category is CPU-bound together, and $\mu$KV is the only member that escapes to the fused path for decode . The only GPU-eligible alternatives are vanilla (no eviction: maximal cache, maximal DRAM traffic, the thermal collapse of Table~\ref{tab:master-wikitext}) and StreamingLLM (score-free, but 1--3/7 NIAH retrieval). Moreover $\mu$KV's core win is \emph{bandwidth reduction}, which is compute-unit-agnostic: a GPU reads the KV cache from the same LPDDR5X, so a smaller attended set lowers DDR heat regardless of where the matmuls run. A faster compute unit spins faster against the same memory wall; it does not remove it.

\paragraph{Portability: iOS and the sensing-fidelity spectrum.} Our measurement methodology requires privileged telemetry --- numeric multi-sensor temperatures, USB-rail $V{\cdot}I$ with charging control, CPU-frequency capping --- that stock iOS does not expose to any app: iOS offers a single 4-level thermal abstraction (\texttt{ProcessInfo.thermalState}), no absolute energy, and no frequency access. The \emph{controller design}, however, ports directly: the watchdog's tier ladder maps one-to-one onto the 4 \texttt{thermalState} levels, and Metal's fused attention presents the same score-visibility conflict that the eviction step's swap resolves (unfused-attention prefill $\to$ fused decode). A preliminary MLX-Swift harness on an iPhone (Instruments: thermal-state timeline, per-core E/P-cluster utilization, relative energy impact) confirms the behavioral endpoints --- sustained tps, time-in-thermal-state, throttle onset --- needed to evaluate the ported controller without numeric sensors. Whether closed-loop cache control degrades gracefully as sensor fidelity drops from a 0.1\,\textdegree C DDR trace to a 2-bit enum is an open question this design is positioned to answer; we leave the port and a second (mid-tier) Android device as the immediate next steps.

\section{Discussion: pick the policy per request}
\label{sec:discuss}

The tables above no longer split along the axis we originally expected. With the count-based dispatcher, retrieval was the workload class where Ada-KV justified its FA-off cost; with the mass-based $\alpha_a$ dispatcher, $\mu$KV matches or beats every score-reading baseline on buried-fact retrieval across all 336 NIAH cells (Table~\ref{tab:phone-niah-prelim}) while remaining $1.4$--$3\times$ faster and cooler, and preliminary LongBench sample-0 F1 shows the same ordering (full $n{=}5$ sweep in progress). The split that survives the data is instead \emph{retrieval vs.\ long continuation}: on prose continuation at 14K context, aggressive eviction costs PPL (2.01 vs 1.15--1.45 full-cache, Table~\ref{tab:master-wikitext}) --- the one axis where the full cache retains a clear quality edge, at $5.5\times$ the wall and twice the energy. The design question is therefore not which evictor wins, but how the phone should set $K$ per request: large for long free-form continuation when cool, small everywhere else --- exactly the admission-control role that the budget ladder $+$ the admission check already occupy (\S\ref{sec:design}).

A request whose output entropy stays high is probably searching for a fact and should not be evicted aggressively; one that commits quickly can be. We are building a per-step entropy gate $K_h(t) = \mathrm{round}(K_{\text{nom}}\,\mu(m_h)\,(1-\tilde{H}(t)))$ that shrinks the budget when normalized output entropy $\tilde{H}(t)$ is high. A server-side study over long-form tasks shows the expected negative correlation between output entropy and attention concentration ($\rho=-0.37$, $p<10^{-55}$), the relationship the gate exploits. A second extension spills evicted cells to flash with an index for linear-time recall, extending the watchdog to flash temperature and I/O depth, so model-internal attention drives both eviction and recall while sensors drive both the frequency cap and the I/O quota.

\textbf{Limitations.} All numbers are on one device; the watchdog thresholds are device-specific. Energy is a disable-charging estimate, not a power-meter measurement, so absolute mAh should be read within about 10\% while the sign and ordering hold. $\mu$KV is faster than the full cache on long prompts and and no longer slower on short ones: in-graph scoring removes the prefill penalty, and only the 290\,ms compaction remains to amortize. All measurements use seed 42; cross-seed variance is in the supplementary material.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

$\mu$KV is a mobile inference pipeline that makes attention-driven KV eviction viable on a phone and measures it end-to-end on a OnePlus 15: a cheap $O(H)$ per-head budget signal, in-graph scoring with sequence-level compaction that keeps the evictor on the fast path, a cliff-insurance frequency watchdog, and thermal-coupled cache control.  08\,tps where $\mu$KV holds 5.18. We honestly report what $\mu$KV does not do --- no configuration of the cache actuator caps peak temperature under external co-load (frequency remains the only lever that does); the symmetric ladder oscillates and is retired in favor of the measured ratchet ($+47\%$ tps, $-23\%$ energy, $-40\%$ time-above-cliff at identical PPL under identical co-load, the 10-arm test); and aggressive eviction still costs perplexity on long free-form continuation even as it preserves retrieval (7/7 NIAH at 4K on every model). The useful takeaway is the substrate, not the trophy: on-device LLM serving should close the loop between attention-driven cache state and hardware sensors per request --- the cache is the phone's \emph{efficiency} actuator, the clock its \emph{temperature} actuator, and the two compose.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} We release the \texttt{eviction\_bench} binary with all six policy implementations, the multi-sensor watchdog daemon, the energy fallback chain, and the host-side harnesses for Wave-11 chunk-pair PPL, NIAH Tier-1, and LongBench at \url{[anonymized URL]}. Phone-side scripts target Android NDK aarch64; host-side LongBench builds against \texttt{llama.cpp} with CUDA. All measurements use seed 42 throughout; the protocol scripts pin DVFS to the performance governor and the kernel cap (1632\,MHz on Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5) before each cell. All raw \texttt{meta.json} and \texttt{stress.csv} artifacts referenced in the tables are included in the release.

